[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `printf("%llx %a %.4f\n", bits(f), f, f)`. Everything observable therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comes from three places: glibc's `scanf` float grammar, glibc's `%a`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc's `%.4f`. The Rust program re-implements all three.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reference commands used for every comparison:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C: `c_src/build/driver` (built with `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Rust: `translation/target/{debug,release}/driver` (`cargo build --release`; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   test harness uses `$CARGO_BIN_EXE_driver`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both were driven as subprocesses with identical stdin, comparing stdout, stderr
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and exit status (including termination by signal).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status when stdout has no reader (SIGPIPE)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Input: any (`1.5\n`), with stdout connected to a pipe whose read end is already
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   closed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C: killed by `SIGPIPE`, wait status signal 13, empty stdout/stderr.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Rust before the fix: exited 0. The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   before `main`, so the failing `write` only returned `EPIPE`, which `driver()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   discards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Cause: a runtime default in Rust that has no counterpart in C, not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation error in the parsing or formatting code.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Fix: `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   as the first statement of `main`, restoring the C disposition. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `sigpipe_when_the_stdout_reader_is_gone`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No other mismatch was observed. The remainder of this file records the behaviors
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that were verified against the C binary, because each of them is a place where a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausible translation would have diverged.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors confirmed against the C binary (no divergence)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%lf")` grammar:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Failed or absent conversion leaves `f` at its initializer, so empty input,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   whitespace-only input, `abc`, `+`, `-`, `.`, `.e5`, `e1` all print
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0 0x0p+0 0.0000` with exit 0 and empty stderr.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Leading whitespace is skipped across newlines (`"\t\n  7.25"` reads `7.25`);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the accepted `isspace` set is the C-locale one (`' '`, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\r`). `\x1c` is not whitespace, so `"\x1c 1"` converts nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Trailing input is simply left unread: `1 2`, `1/3`, `1.5\xff` all read the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   first number.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - An `e`/`p` exponent with no digits backtracks: `1e`, `1e+`, `0x1p`, `0x1.8p+q`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   read the significand only.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `0x` with nothing usable after it is a *matching failure*, so the sign is lost
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   too: `-0x` yields `+0.0` (bits `0`), while `-0x.` succeeds through strtod
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   backtracking and yields `-0.0` (bits `8000000000000000`). This asymmetry is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   real and is reproduced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `inf` and `infinity` are accepted case-insensitively, but a partially typed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `infi`, `infin`, `infini`, `infinit` is a matching failure (once a fourth `i`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is consumed the full spelling is required), whereas `infx`, `inf.`, `inf1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   read `inf`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan`, `nan()`, `nan(1)`, `nan(0x5)`, `nan(abc)`, unterminated `nan(123` all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   produce the default quiet NaN `7ff8000000000000`; this glibc does **not**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   install the payload. The sign is kept: `-nan` gives `fff8000000000000`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Overflow gives `±inf` and underflow gives `±0.0` with the sign preserved
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`-1e-99999` → `8000000000000000`), and `0e<huge>` stays zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%a` formatting:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Zero prints `0x0p+0` / `-0x0p+0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Normals print `0x1.<hex>p±<dec>` with trailing mantissa zeros removed and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `.` dropped when the mantissa is zero (`0x1p+3`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Subnormals are **not** renormalized: glibc keeps the raw mantissa with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   leading `0` digit and a fixed exponent, e.g. `5e-324` → `0x0.0000000000001p-1022`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1e-320` → `0x0.00000000007e8p-1022`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Non-finite values print `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`, `-nan`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%.4f` formatting:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The exact binary value is rounded, ties to even. Exact ties exist only for odd
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   multiples of 2^-5: `0.03125` → `0.0312`, `0.09375` → `0.0938`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1048575.03125` → `1048575.0312`. Rust's `{:.4}` agrees.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Large values print their full expansion (309 digits for `1e308`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0x1.fffffffffffffp+1023`), matching glibc digit for digit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Non-finite values print `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`, `-nan`; `-0.0` prints `-0.0000`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%llx`: prints the raw 64-bit pattern with no padding, so the sign bit of `-0.0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and of NaN is visible; this is why signed zero and NaN sign had to be exact.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage actually executed
